course: dictionary
lesson: 15951
title: Walking unsteadily
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Unsteadily means in a wobbly, shaky way, not firm or balanced. It is an adverb, so it tells you how something is done. «Mia walked unsteadily across the wet deck.» The action is walked, and unsteadily tells us how she walked.
explain: The words around a new word give you clues. The deck is wet and Mia is walking across it, so she might slip. Ask yourself what word would fit. Swap in shakily or wobbly and check that the sentence still makes sense.
explain: People walk, stand or rise unsteadily when they are tired, ill, dizzy or just learning. «The newborn foal rose unsteadily onto its thin legs.» A newborn foal has not yet learned to balance, so its movements are shaky.
fact: Unsteadily means in a wobbly, shaky way, not firm or balanced.
fact: Unsteadily is an adverb: it tells you how an action happens.
fact: «Walk unsteadily» means to walk in a wobbly way.
fact: «Rise unsteadily» means to get up in a shaky way.
fact: If someone is walking unsteadily, they may need help, so stay close and be ready to support them.
exercise: «After the flu, Sam stood up unsteadily.» How did Sam stand up? | In a loud, angry voice | In a fast, bouncing way | *In a wobbly, shaky way
exercise: «The toddler took her first steps, walking unsteadily across the rug.» Find and copy the word that tells how she walked. | walking | first | *unsteadily | rug
exercise: Ellie says, «Don't walk unsteadily on the ice. Hold my hand.» Why does Ellie ask this? | Because the ice is too bright to see | Because ice is warm and soft | Because she wants to have a race | *Because wobbly walking can lead to a fall
test: «The dizzy man rose unsteadily from his chair.» Which word has the same meaning as unsteadily? | *Shakily | Happily | Firmly | Quietly
test: Which person is most likely to walk unsteadily? | A child in a fast race | A pupil reading a book at a desk | A runner who is warming up calmly | *A dizzy man who has just spun round and round
exam: «The old man walked unsteadily up the steps, so his granddaughter took his arm.» Why did she take his arm? | Because the steps were made of gold | *Because he might fall | Because she wanted to race him to the top | Because she was tired of walking
